WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.049
Let's talk about some of the core fundamentals of Sequencer.

2
00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.610
So the first question is, what is a Sequencer?

3
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:08.730
What is Sequencer?

4
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:14.410
So Sequencer is a non-linear editing tool inside of Unreal that allows us to assemble

5
00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:20.530
content, whether it's content that's currently in our game or on our map, or to be able

6
00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:25.329
to create spawnable assets, which we'll talk about here in just a second.

7
00:00:25.329 --> 00:00:26.469
We can add audio.

8
00:00:26.469 --> 00:00:28.030
We can add camera cuts.

9
00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:33.630
We essentially can create, for lack of better terms, a complete feature film within the editor

10
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:38.469
itself without ever having to go outside and use any other DCC programs.

11
00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:43.469
Additionally, as it relates to games, we can actually use Sequencer to create in-game

12
00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:44.670
cutscenes.

13
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.869
We can use it for loading screens.

14
00:00:46.869 --> 00:00:49.149
We can use it for player showcases.

15
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:54.090
Again, Sequencer is not just about creating film, but basically allows us to assemble

16
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.810
all of these components in a, for lack of better terms, a pre-scripted way to get the

17
00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.770
visual results that we want.

18
00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:05.530
Now one thing to cover is that we're going to be talking about terminology about film,

19
00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:09.730
sequence, shots, nested sequences, master sequences, et cetera.

20
00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.730
Fundamentally, all of these are going to be using a sequence from inside of the

21
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:14.730
editor.

22
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:18.609
And it's important to understand that a sequence, you don't have to have a different type

23
00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:22.849
of sequence or a different type of piece of content to create these.

24
00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:27.650
We're going to be using the same sequence and essentially it's just a container.

25
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.049
Really the important part to remember is how you use that container that really changes

26
00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:36.569
its function, which again is going to allow us to create shots, sequences, graduated

27
00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:40.370
sequences, master sequences, and then finally everything propagated up into what we'll

28
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.969
just effectively call our film.

29
00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:47.890
A couple pieces of terminology that you need to understand when it comes to Sequencer is

30
00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.609
we can target content that's already in our map.

31
00:01:51.609 --> 00:01:57.890
So for example, if we have certain interactable components or we have certain NPCs, characters,

32
00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.129
we will call those possessables.

33
00:01:59.129 --> 00:02:04.930
And essentially it just allows us to target whatever those assets are and inside of our

34
00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:10.150
Sequencer make changes, whether it's translation, attribute changes, et cetera.

35
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:14.289
Fundamentally speaking, those are already in our world, which brings us to our second

36
00:02:14.289 --> 00:02:17.310
type, which is a spawnable.

37
00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.870
Level assets really only exist inside of the level sequence.

38
00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.629
And we know that identified by this lightning bolt icon.

39
00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:28.009
So essentially what we could do is we could have our world that's built for our game

40
00:02:28.009 --> 00:02:29.849
that our player is playing through.

41
00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:32.990
And we have an in-game cut scene, which we'll actually build here in just a little

42
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.150
bit.

43
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:39.110
And in that cut scene, we may have an NPC that exists only for that cut scene.

44
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:44.069
So rather than have that character just floating somewhere in the world during our sequence

45
00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:47.189
during playback, we can have that object spawn in.

46
00:02:47.189 --> 00:02:50.710
And then when the sequence is done, unload and spawn out.
